<comment datatype='string'>The aerobic &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=MONOMER-12132&quot;&gt;phenylacetate-coA ligase&lt;/a&gt;, the enzyme catalyzing the first step in the aerobic degradation of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=PHENYLACETATE&quot;&gt;phenylacetate&lt;/a&gt;, has been purified from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-59406&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Aromatoleum evansii&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and characterized.
The enzyme was induced after aerobic growth in a chemically defined medium containing &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=PHENYLACETATE&quot;&gt;phenylacetate&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=PHE&quot;&gt;L-phenylalanine&lt;/a&gt; as the sole carbon source. Enzymatic activity was detected very early in the exponential phase of growth.
&lt;p&gt;
The biochemical and molecular characteristics of this enzyme are very different from those of the isoenzyme catalyzing the same reaction under anaerobic conditions in the same bacterium [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10629172&quot;&gt;ElSaid00&lt;/a&gt;].
</comment>
